                %a{:href => "#examples"} Examples
              %ul
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#dirac_valuation"} Dirac valuation
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#simple_valuations"} Simple valuations
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#borel_measures"} Borel measures
              %li
                %a{:href => "#measuretheoretic_structures_and_properties"} Measure-theoretic structures and properties
              %ul
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#pushforward"} Pushforward
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#joints_marginals_and_products"} Joints, marginals and products
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#null_sets_and_support"} Null sets and support
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#integration"} Integration
              %li
                %a{:href => "#monads_of_valuations"} Monads of valuations
              %li
                %a{:href => "#extending_valuations_to_measures"} Extending valuations to measures
              %ul
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#particular_cases"} Particular cases
              %li
                %a{:href => "#see_also"} See also
              %li
                %a{:href => "#references"} References
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            A
            %strong valuation
            is a construction analogous to that of a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/measure"} measure
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
            rather than on a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/measurable+space"} measurable space
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+mathematics"} constructive mathematics
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/point-free+topology"} point-free topology
          %p
            On most spaces of interest for measure theory and probability (such as
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/metric+spaces"} metric spaces
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#extending_valuations_to_measures"} below
          %h2#definition Definition
          %h3#valuations_on_lattices Valuations on lattices
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
            be a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/distributive+lattice"} distributive lattice
            with a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bottom"} bottom
            element
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ⊥
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bottom
            %strong valuation
            or
            %strong evaluation
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/map"} map
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ν
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nu
            from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
            into the space of non-negative
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/one-sided+real+number"} lower reals
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %strong> Monotonicity
                \: for all
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ,
                      %mi y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x,y
                in
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi L
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ≤
                      %mi y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x\le y
                implies
                = succeed ";" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi ν
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi x
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo ≤
                        %mi ν
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi y
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nu(x)\le\nu(y)
            %li
              %p
                %strong Strictness
                (or
                = succeed "):" do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unitality"} unitality
                = succeed ";" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi ν
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mo ⊥
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo =
                        %mn 0
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nu(\bottom)=0
            %li
              %p
                %strong> Modularity
                \: for all
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ,
                      %mi y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x,y
                in
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi L
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ν
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo +
                  %mi ν
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi ν
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ∨
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo +
                  %mi ν
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ∧
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\nu(x) + \nu(y) = \nu(x \vee y) + \nu(x \wedge y) .
          %p
            Moreover, we call a valuation
            %em continuous
            if the following property holds, which is an instance of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Scott+topology"} Scott continuity
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tau-additive+measure"}
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi τ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tau
                \-additivity
          %ul
            %li
              %strong> Continuity
              \: for every
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/directed+set"} directed
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/net"} net
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mi λ
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %mrow
                        %mi λ
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi Λ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{x_\lambda\}_{\lambda\in\Lambda}
              in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
              admitting a supremum,
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ν
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                  %munder
                    %mi sup
                    %mi λ
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mi λ
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                  %mo =
                  %munder
                    %mi sup
                    %mi λ
                  %mi ν
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mi λ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\nu \big( \sup_{\lambda} x_\lambda \big) = \sup_\lambda \nu(x_\lambda) .
          %p
            Note that, differently from measures, there is no explicit mention of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complements"} complements
            = succeed "-additivity," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%CF%84-additivity"} τ-additivity
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/correspondence+between+measure+and+valuation+theory"} correspondence between measure and valuation theory
            for more on this.
          %h3#valuations_on_locales_and_topological_spaces Valuations on locales and topological spaces
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
            be a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locale"} locale
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
            is by definition a valuation on its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/frame"} frame
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒪
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi L
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{O}(L)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
            is a valuation on the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lattice+of+open+subsets"} lattice of its open sets
          %p
            Valuations on locales are used in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos+approach+to+quantum+mechanics"} topos approach to quantum mechanics
            and the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bohr+topos"} Bohr topos
          %h2#examples Examples
          %h3#dirac_valuation Dirac valuation
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            be a topological space, and let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x\in X
            be a point. The
            %strong Dirac valuation
            at
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi δ
                      %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta_x
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                  %mo ⊆
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U\subseteq X
            to
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi δ
                    %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ≔
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mrow
                    %mo {
                    %mrow
                      %mtable{:columnalign => "left left", :displaystyle => "false"}
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mn 1
                          %mtd
                            %mi x
                            %mo ∈
                            %mi U
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mn 0
                          %mtd
                            %mi x
                            %mo ∉
                            %mi U
                            %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\delta_x(U) \;\coloneqq\;
                  \\begin{cases}
                  1 &amp; x\in U \\
                  0 &amp; x\notin U.
                  \\end{cases}
          %p
            These are the valuation analogue of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dirac+measures"} Dirac measures
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/valuation+monads"} valuation monads
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/neighborhood+filters"} neighborhood filters
          %h3#simple_valuations Simple valuations
          %p
            On a topological space, a
            %strong simple valuation
            is a finite convex (or linear) combination of Dirac valuations, i.e. a valuation in the form
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ν
                  %mo =
                  %munderover
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mo =
                      %mn 1
                    %mi n
                  %msub
                    %mi α
                    %mi i
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %msub
                    %mi δ
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mi i
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\nu = \sum_{i=1}^n \alpha_i\,\delta_{x_i} ,
          %p
            for
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mi i
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_i\in X
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi α
                      %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha_i
          %h3#borel_measures Borel measures
          %p
            For more on this, see
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tau-additive+measure"}
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi τ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tau
                \-additive measure
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            be a topological space, and let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi μ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu
            be a measure defined on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Borel+subset"}
              Borel
              = succeed "-algebra" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi σ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi μ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu
            to the open subsets of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is a valuation. The valuation is continuous if and only if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi μ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu
            is
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tau-additive+measure"}
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi τ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tau
                \-additive
          %p
            The converse problem of whether a valuation is the restriction of a Borel measure is more difficult, see
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#extending_valuations_to_measures"} below
          %h2#measuretheoretic_structures_and_properties Measure-theoretic structures and properties
          %p
            Valuations share a number of constructions which are similar to those for
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/measures"} measures
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+spaces"} topological spaces
          %h3#pushforward Pushforward
          %p
            Given spaces
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            and a continuous map
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo :
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f:X\to Y
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ν
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nu
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            to a valuation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mo *
                  %mi ν
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_*\nu
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            in the following way. For every open set
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                    %mo ⊆
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U\subseteq Y
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mo *
                  %mi ν
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≔
                  %mi ν
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi f
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  f_*\nu (U) \coloneqq \nu(f^{-1}(U)) .
          %p
            The assignment gives a well-defined valuation, which is continuous if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ν
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nu
            is continuous. We call
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mo *
                  %mi ν
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_*\nu
            the
            %strong pushforward valuation
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ν
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nu
            along
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
          %p
            Compare with the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pushforward+measure"} analogous construction for measures
          %p
            This construction allows to makes the assignment
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monad"} monad
            (see
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monads+of+probability%2C+measures+and+valuations#functor_+unit_and_multiplication"} measure monads
            for more on this).
          %h3#joints_marginals_and_products Joints, marginals and products
          %p
            Given a valuation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ν
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nu
            on a product space
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo ×
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X\times Y
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo ×
                  %mi Y
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X\times Y\to X
            the
            %strong marginal
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ν
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nu
            on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ν
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nu
            to the ordered pair of its marginals can be seen as an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+functor"} oplax monoidal structure
            of the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monads+of+probability%2C+measures+and+valuations"} valuation monad
          %p
            Given valuations
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ν
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nu
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ρ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \rho
            on
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo ×
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X\times Y
            which have
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ν
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nu
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ρ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \rho
            as their marginals. Any such valuation on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo ×
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X\times Y
            is called a
            %strong joint valuation
            or
            %strong coupling
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ν
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nu
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ρ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \rho
          %p
            Between the many joints of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ν
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nu
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ρ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \rho
            there is always a canonical choice, namely the
            %strong product valuation
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ν
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi ρ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nu\otimes\rho
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/probability+theory"} probability theory
            %span.newWikiWord
              independence
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/independence"} ?
            between
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ν
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nu
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ρ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \rho
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/basis"} basis
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/product+topology"} product topology
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo ×
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X\times Y
            given by sets in the form
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                  %mo ×
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U\times V
            for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                  %mo ⊆
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U\subseteq X
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                  %mo ⊆
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V\subseteq Y
            open, the product valuation is given by
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ν
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi ρ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi U
                  %mo ×
                  %mi V
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≔
                  %mi ν
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mi ρ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi V
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\nu\otimes\rho (U\times V) \coloneqq \nu(U)\cdot\rho(V).
          %p
            Note that this is not enough to define a valuation a priori. For continuous valuations, however, this turns out to be the well-defined, as proven for example in
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Heckmann96"} Heckmann ‘96
          %p
            The assignment to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ν
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nu
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ρ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \rho
            of their product valuation can be seen as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+functor"} lax monoidal structure
            of the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monads+of+probability%2C+measures+and+valuations"} valuation monad
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monads+of+probability%2C+measures+and+valuations#monoidal_structure"} monads of probability, measures and valuations#monoidal_structure
          %h3#null_sets_and_support Null sets and support
          %p
            Valuations admit a notion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/support"} support
            similar to that of measures. In particular, continuous valuations, just as
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tau-additive+measure"}
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi τ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tau
                \-additive measures
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ν
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nu
            be a valuation on a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locale"} locale
            or
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
            an open set of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            (i.e. an element of the corresponding
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/frame"} frame
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
            is a
            %strong null
            or
            %strong measure zero set
            for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ν
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nu
            if
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ν
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nu(U)=0
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complement"} complement
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+subspace#locales"} closed subspace
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            = succeed "." do
              %strong full measure
          %p
            Since a finite union of null sets is null, null sets form a directed net in the frame. Therefore, if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ν
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nu
            is a continuous valuation, it admits a unique maximal null open set. The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complement"} complement
            of this set, which is the largest closed subspace of full measure, is called the
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/support"} support
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ν
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nu
          %p
            The support induces a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monad#the_bicategory_of_monads"} morphism of monads
            (see
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#support"} Fritz-Perrone-Rezagholi
          %h3#integration Integration
          %p
            There is an integration theory for valuation analogous to that of measures, where the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+sets"} open sets
            play the role of the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/measurable+sets"} measurable sets
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semicontinuous+functions"} semicontinuous functions
            play the role of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/measurable+functions"} measurable functions
            (see also
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/correspondence+between+measure+and+valuation+theory"} correspondence between measure and valuation theory
            = succeed "," do
              %strong lower integration
          %p
            The way to define integration, mutatis mutandis, parallels usual
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lebesgue+integral"} Lebesgue integral
            construction. We sketch the construction for the case of topological spaces.
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ν
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nu
            be a valuation on a space
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Given an open set
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi U
                        %mo ⊆
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U\subseteq X
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mn 1
                        %mi U
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1_U
                its
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/indicator+function"} indicator function
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ∫
                      %msub
                        %mn 1
                        %mi U
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mi d
                      %mi ν
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mo ≔
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi ν
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi U
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo .
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      \\int 1_U \, d\nu \;\coloneqq\; \nu(U) .
            %li
              %p
                A
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simple+function"} simple
                lower semicontinuous function is a lower semicontinuous function assuming only finitely many values. Such functions can be expressed (nonuniquely) as finite positive linear combinations of indicator functions:
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mo =
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %munder
                        %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                        %mi i
                      %msub
                        %mi r
                        %mi i
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %msub
                        %mn 1
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi U
                            %mi i
                      %mo .
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      f \;=\; \sum_i r_i \, 1_{U_i} .
              %p
                We define the integral of a simple
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
                as
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ∫
                      %mi f
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mi d
                      %mi ν
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mo ≔
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %munder
                        %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                        %mi i
                      %msub
                        %mi r
                        %mi i
                      %mi ν
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi U
                        %mi i
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo .
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      \\int f \, d\nu \;\coloneqq\; \sum_{i}r_i \nu(U_i) .
              %p
                This is well-defined, i.e.~it depends only on
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
                and not on the particular way of expressing
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
                as a linear combination of indicators.
            %li
              %p
                Every lower semicontinuous function can be written as pointwise directed supremum of simple lower semicontinuous functions. So suppose
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi g
                      %mo :
                      %mi X
                      %mo →
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mn 0
                      %mo ,
                      %mn ∞
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g:X\to[0,\infty]
                is lower semicontinuous. Take an increasing net
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi g
                        %mi α
                      %msub
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mrow
                          %mi α
                          %mo ∈
                          %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (g_\alpha)_{\alpha\in A}
                of nonnegative simple lower semicontinuous functions. Then we define
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ∫
                      %mi g
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mi d
                      %mi ν
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mo ≔
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %munder
                        %mi sup
                        %mi α
                      %mo ∫
                      %msub
                        %mi g
                        %mi α
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mi d
                      %mi ν
                      %mo ,
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      \\int g \, d\nu \;\coloneqq\; \sup_\alpha \int g_\alpha\,d\nu ,
              %p
                where the integral on the right is the one defined above for simple functions, and the supremum on the right is either the one of real numbers (or lower real numbers), or
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} +
                        %mn ∞
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} +\infty
          %p
            This integral satisfies analogous properties to the Lebesgue integral, such as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+map"} linearity
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Scott+continuity"} Scott continuity
            (cfr. the
            %span.newWikiWord
              sequential monotone continuity
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/monotone+convergence+theorem"} ?
            of the Lebesgue integral).
          %h2#monads_of_valuations Monads of valuations
          %p
            Just as there are several
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monads+of+measures"} monads of measures
            (such as the
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Giry+monad"} Giry monad
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monads"} monads
            of valuations. The most famous are
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                The
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extended+probabilistic+powerdomain"} extended probabilistic powerdomain
                on the
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Top"} category of topological spaces
                = succeed "." do
                  %a{:href => "#Heckmann96"} Heckmann 96
            %li
              %p
                The
                = succeed "," do
                  %span.newWikiWord
                    valuation monad on locales
                    %a{:href => "/nlab/new/valuation+monad+on+locales"} ?
                = succeed "." do
                  %a{:href => "#vmonad"} Steve Vickers
            %li
              %p
                The
                %span.newWikiWord
                  probabilistic powerdomain
                  %a{:href => "/nlab/new/probabilistic+powerdomain"} ?
                on the category of
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dcpo"} dcpos
          %p
            See also the list at
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monads+of+probability%2C+measures%2C+and+valuations"} monads of probability, measures, and valuations
          %h2#extending_valuations_to_measures Extending valuations to measures
          %p
            As we have seen
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#borel_measures"} above
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Borel+measure"} Borel measure
            always restricts to a valuation. It is natural to ask the converse question of whether a valuation can always be extended to a Borel measure. In general, the answer is negative. In the case of
            %em continuous
            valuations, however, one would expect that in many cases the valuation can be extended to a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tau-additive+measure"}
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi τ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tau
                \-additive Borel measure
          %p
            The question is known, for example, to be true on all regular Hausdorff (
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi T
                    %mn 3
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_3
            ) spaces:
          #ExtensionOfValuationToBorelMeasure.num_theorem
            %h6#theorem Theorem
            %p
              A locally finite continuous valuation on a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/regular+topological+space"} regular topological space
              extends uniquely to a regular τ-smooth
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Borel+measure"} Borel measure
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+compact"} locally compact
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sober+space"} sober space
              extends uniquely to a τ-smooth
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Borel+measure"} Borel measure
          %p
            (
            %a{:href => "#Manilla02"}> Manilla 02, Theorems 4.4 and 4.12
            )
          %p
            This includes in particular every
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/metric+space"} metric space
          %p The more general question of whether one can extend a finite continuous valuation to a Borel measure on any sober space, at the present time, is still open.
          %h3#particular_cases Particular cases
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Every Dirac valuation can be extended to the corresponding
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dirac+measure"} Dirac measure
            %li
              %p
                The pushforward of an extendable valuation along a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuous+map"} continuous map
                is again extendable, and the resulting
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/measure"} measure
                is the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pushforward+measure"} pushforward measure
                of the extension. (Equivalently, the restriction of measures to valuations is a
                = succeed ")." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+transformation"} natural transformation
            %li
              %p
                The above specializes to the fact that marginals of extendable valuations are extendable, and the resulting measure is the
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/marginal+measure"} marginal measure
            %li
              %p
                Somewhat conversely, the product of extendable valuations is extendable, and the resulting measure is the
                %span.newWikiWord
                  product measure
                  %a{:href => "/nlab/new/product+measure"} ?
                of the extensions.
            %li
              %p
                The integration, given by the multiplication map
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi E
                      %mo :
                      %mi P
                      %mi P
                      %mi X
                      %mo →
                      %mi P
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E: P P X \to P X
                of the
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extended+probabilistic+powerdomain"} extended probabilistic powerdomain
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monad#the_bicategory_of_monads"} morphism of monads
                exhibiting
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%CF%84-additive+measures"} τ-additive measures
                as a submonad of valuations. (See also
                = succeed ".)" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extended+probabilistic+powerdomain#the_measure_monad_on_top"} the measure monad on Top
          %h2#see_also See also
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monads+of+probability%2C+measures%2C+and+valuations"} monads of probability, measures, and valuations
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/correspondence+between+measure+and+valuation+theory"} correspondence between measure and valuation theory
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monad"}> monad
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Giry+monad"} Giry monad
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monads+in+computer+science"} monads in computer science
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extended+probabilistic+powerdomain"}> extended probabilistic powerdomain
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %span.newWikiWord
                  probabilistic powerdomain
                  %a{:href => "/nlab/new/probabilistic+powerdomain"} ?
              %span.newWikiWord
                valuation monad on locales
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/valuation+monad+on+locales"} ?
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/probability+valuation"} probability valuation
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sigma-continuous+valuation"}> sigma-continuous valuation
              ,
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sigma-continuous+probability+valuation"} sigma-continuous probability valuation
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/content+%28measure+theory%29"}> content (measure theory)
              ,
              %span.newWikiWord
                probability content
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/probability+content"} ?
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/measure"}> measure
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%CF%84-additive+measure"} τ-additive measure
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/probability+measure"} probability measure
          %h2#references References
          %ul
            %li#Heckmann96
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Reinhold+Heckmann"}> Reinhold Heckmann
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Spaces of valuations
                %strong 806
                1
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Papers on General Topology and Applications
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1749-6632.1996.tb49168.x"} doi:10.1111/j.1749-6632.1996.tb49168.x
                %a{:href => "http://citeseerx.ist.psu.edu/viewdoc/download?doi=10.1.1.45.5845&rep=rep1&type=pdf"} pdf
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ]
            %li
              %p
                Mauricio Alvarez-Manilla, Achin Jung,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Klaus+Keimel"} Klaus Keimel
                = succeed "," do
                  %em The probabilistic powerdomain for stably compact spaces
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.tcs.2004.06.021"} doi:10.1016/j.tcs.2004.06.021
            %li#algebras
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jean+Goubault-Larrecq"} Jean Goubault-Larrecq
                and Xiaodong Jia,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Algebras of the extended probabilistic powerdomain monad
              %p
                (
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.entcs.2019.07.015"}> doi:10.1016/j.entcs.2019.07.015
                )
            %li#support
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tobias+Fritz"}> Tobias Fritz
                ,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Paolo+Perrone"} Paolo Perrone
                and Sharwin Rezagholi,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Probability, valuations, hyperspace: Three monads on Top and the support as a morphism
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1910.03752"} arXiv:1910.03752
          %p For valuations on locales, see
          %ul
            %li#vmonad
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Steve+Vickers"}> Steve Vickers
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em A monad of valuation locales
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "https://www.cs.bham.ac.uk/~sjv/Riesz.pdf"} pdf
          %p
            For the theory of
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#integration"} integration over valuations
          %ul
            %li#kirch
              %p
                Olaf Kirch,
                %em Bereiche und Bewertungen
                (in German), Master Thesis, Technische Hochschule Darmstadt, 1993 (
                %a{:href => "http://fb04286.mathematik.tu-darmstadt.de/fbereiche/logik/research/Domains/papers/kirch/diplom.ps.gz"}> ps.gz
                )
            %li#jung
              %p
                Achim Jung,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Stably compact spaces and the probabilistic powerspace construction
                = succeed ")." do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.entcs.2004.10.001"} doi:10.1016/j.entcs.2004.10.001
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Thierry+Coquand"} Thierry Coquand
                and
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bas+Spitters"} Bas Spitters
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Integrals and Valuations
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/0808.1522"} arXiv:0808.1522
          %p Integration on locales can be found in
          %ul
            %li#vintegral
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Steve+Vickers"}> Steve Vickers
              ,
              = succeed "," do
